## Why This Is Asked

Webhook delivery from external providers is inherently unreliable: duplicates, retries, and reordering are common. This question probes practical test strategies that ensure data integrity and idempotency across asynchronous paths.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and deduping
- Signature verification (HMAC)
- Retry/backoff handling with 429/503
- Out-of-order event handling
- End-to-end ledger consistency and downstream effects

## Code Example

```javascript
// Signature verification (example)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function verifySignature(payload, signature, secret) {
  const hmac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret);
  hmac.update(payload, 'utf8');
  return hmac.digest('hex') === signature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate out-of-order delivery and duplicates in an automated test suite?
- What changes would you make to migrate from at-least-once to exactly-once processing in this webhook path?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests reliability of composite APIs with dependent services.

## Key Concepts
- Downstream latency, timeouts, partial failures, circuit breakers
- Idempotent retries, exponential backoff, observability
- End-to-end timing and schema validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test using jest + nock
const nock = require('nock');
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');
test('partial data when one downstream times out', async () => {
  nock('http://sales').get('/daily').delay(3000).reply(200, {sales: 1000});
  nock('http://customers').get('/daily').reply(200, {customers: 50});
  const res = await request(app).get('/reports/daily');
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(200);
  expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('sales');
  expect(res.headers).toHaveProperty('x-downstream-warn');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under sustained latency?
- What metrics would you validate in your observability stack?
